   "text": {
    "L1": "Area tells us how much flat space a shape covers. We count the unit squares inside it.",
    "L2": "A common mistake is to confuse area with perimeter. Area counts squares inside; perimeter counts units around the edge.",
    "L3": "This 4-square by 3-square patch covers 12 squares. So its area is 12 square units.",
    "L4": "For an uneven shape, count full squares first. Pair two half squares to make one whole square.",
    "L5": "Leave out pieces that are less than half a square. In the worked example, 9 full squares and 6 halves give about 12 square units.",
    "L6": "Shapes can have the same perimeter but different areas. A 1 by 5 strip covers 5 squares, while a 3 by 3 square covers 9.",
    "L7": "Now count 6 full squares and 4 half squares. The four halves make two whole squares.",
    "L8": "Choose the area of this shape. Tap your answer."
   },
